    "netmedex_hybrid_rag": "**Mechanistic chain from CFTR loss to intestinal inflammation**\n\n* **CFTR loss → altered intestinal milieu / dysbiosis**  \n  * *Animal*: Whole‑body CFTR‑KO mice develop gut dysbiosis (↑Proteobacteria, ↓diversity) and increased intestinal permeability; intestinal‑specific Cftr KO mice show the same pattern (PMID 37546931; PMID 38073491).  \n  * *Human*: CF patients universally exhibit gut dysbiosis (reduced diversity, pro‑inflammatory taxa) and chronic low‑grade inflammation (PMID 35160099; PMID 36600119).  \n\n* **Dysbiosis → activation of inflammatory signaling**  \n  * *Animal*: In CFTR‑KO mice, dysbiotic microbiota correlates with elevated fecal calprotectin and mucosal neutrophil/macrophage infiltration; restoring gut CFTR (CFTR‑KO‑GC) eliminates both dysbiosis and inflammation (PMID 38554847).  \n  * *Human association*: Higher abundance of pro‑inflammatory microbes aligns with raised fecal calprotectin and systemic cytokines in CF (PMID 35160099).  \n\n* **CFTR loss → direct NF‑κB activation (independent of microbiota)**  \n  * *In‑vitro*: CFTR knock‑down in Caco‑2 cells triggers β‑catenin loss, NF‑κB nuclear translocation, and up‑regulation of COX‑2/PGE₂; rescuing β‑catenin suppresses NF‑κB activation (PMID 27588407).  \n  * *Animal*: ΔF508 mouse intestine mirrors the in‑vitro findings—suppressed β‑catenin and heightened NF‑κB/COX‑2 signaling (PMID 27588407).  \n\n* **Inflammatory signaling → measurable intestinal inflammation**  \n  * *Human*: CF cohorts show consistently elevated fecal calprotectin, S100A12 and lactoferrin, confirming ongoing intestinal inflammation (PMID 35160099).  \n  * *Animal*: CFTR‑KO mice display peri‑intestinal neutrophil influx, cytokine elevation, and histologic inflammation; these resolve when gut permeability is normalized (PMID 38554847).  \n\n* **Testable hypothesis linking hops**  \n  * Restoring CFTR function in intestinal epithelium (e.g., with CFTR modulators) should normalize β‑catenin levels, reduce NF‑κB activity, correct dysbiosis, and lower fecal calprotectin—predicted to be observable in both iCftr‑KO mice and CF patients undergoing modulator therapy.  \n\n**Conclusion:** Multiple human, mouse and cell‑culture studies provide direct evidence that CFTR deficiency reshapes the gut environment, drives dysbiosis, and triggers β‑catenin‑dependent NF‑κB activation, collectively producing intestinal inflammation."
